    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The current judicial landscape regarding the Second Amendment is largely defined by the 2008 *District of Columbia v. Hansford* decision and the 2022 *New York State Rifle & Pistol Assn. v. Bruen* ruling, which established a \"text, history, and tradition\" originalist framework that the majority applied in *Bruen* for firearm regulation. This approach has marginalized \"living constitutionalist\" methodologies that sought to infer broader individual rights from general liberties or evolving societal standards, as seen in the dissenting opinions of Justices Sotomayor and Kagan in cases explicitly rejecting the \"deletion of the right\" or \"inherent rights\" approaches. The Supreme Court has consistently signaled that current regulatory debates must be resolved by examining what the narrow statute and its 18th-century and state history forbid or permit, effectively excluding ongoing judicial interpretation from the modern rights discourse. Consequently, future legal battles will likely hinge on the specific historical scope of \"peaceful\" versus \"unlawful\" uses of arms, making historical evidence the primary determinant of constitutional validity rather than moral or policy considerations.",
