    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Living constitutionalism is grounded in the premise that the Constitution's abstract language and broad principles, such as \"Liberty,\" must be interpreted through the evolving values of the society they govern. Proponents argue that this approach avoids the \"slippery slope\" of originalism by focusing on contemporary common sense and current societal practices, effectively allowing the text to remain a living document rather than a static artifact. This methodology was prominently argued by legal scholars like Alexander Bickel and in practice by Justices like the late Thurgood Marshall, who emphasized justice and equality over rigid historical adherence to clarify individual rights.",
